  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was softer than the prior Thursday at both locations, but the pattern differs meaningfully and calendar timing matters here\u2014Memorial Day was Monday, so we're in post-holiday booking normalization.\n\n**Port Washington** is the concern. Revenue down 15% on a 6-groomer day ($457/groomer vs. trailing-30d baseline of $511), which signals real productivity loss, not just capacity. Add-on attach collapsed from 59% to 38%\u2014that's a upselling discipline issue at the front desk, not a scheduling problem. Retail also underperformed sharply (down 28%), suggesting customers are less engaged or the team isn't merchandising aggressively post-holiday. Month-to-date you're actually tracking ahead of DOW-adjusted expectation by 1.8 percentage points, so May overall is solid; this is a single-day operational dip. The bright spot: YTD you're up 56% in revenue and 57% in appointments versus last year\u2014that's the real story and worth the team's attention.\n\n**Hicksville** is performing *better than expected*. Revenue down only 9% on fewer groomer-days, and critically, you're *beating* your DOW-adjusted May expectation by 1.6 points. Retail is weaker (down 35%), but that's consistent with Port Washington and likely post-holiday noise. However, Hicksville's scheduling remains tight: tomorrow you have only 2 groomers. With a $384/groomer-day baseline and tomorrow's skeleton crew, you're leaving money on the table.\n\nBoth stores show retail softness and add-on drift\u2014front-of-house needs to tighten upsell discipline. Port Washington's visit-cadence shows 15.3% in the 12+ week bucket (slightly elevated churn risk); Hicksville at 8.4% is healthier.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Today Port Washington runs 6 groomers with 7.0h bather support; Hicksville runs 2 groomers with zero bather hours; next 7 days Port Washington has 29 groomer-days scheduled versus Hicksville's 11\u2014verify if Hicksville's staffing reflects demand or constraint."
